{"parentUuid":"dac23a9b-0f6c-4c0a-bef8-859c092a766a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_01JUzTRyvYGzaTP9UysuHNPb","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7840a0b6-7c28-4554-a34c-33eff58eda34","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:31:59.900Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"7840a0b6-7c28-4554-a34c-33eff58eda34","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEkkq4awSWUjhBqEV76q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Real article cards and video posters use remote https images, which would just render blank under the liquid canvas effect — that's a genuine issue since article cards are a core card type. One fix is intercepting image requests in Electron's main process to inject CORS headers, paired with setting crossOrigin on the renderer's remote images.\n\nThis doesn't trigger a new network call — it's the same request, just with cookies stripped and the CORS check satisfied via the injected header, which is a workaround I recall being commonly used in Electron apps.\n\nAs for whether it violates our data access policy, the remote images are already being fetched as part of the normal flow, and sanitized HTML images without the crossorigin attribute won't even be affected by the ACAO header since they're loaded in no-cors mode anyway. The remaining concern is whether a cached no-cors response for the same URL gets reused and whether the webRequest header injection re-applies to cache hits.\n\nI'm also realizing the bigger issue: since the entire grid is rendered via a layoutsubtree canvas, if a cross-origin image fails the CORS check entirely, the card wouldn't just look wrong under the glass effect -- it would show completely blank in the main view, since only canvas-drawn content is visible for layoutsubtree children. That's a much worse regression than any under-glass visual glitch, so I need to verify whether this canvas-painting behavior is actually accurate before deciding how to proceed.\n\nRight, the note confirms rendering small thumbnails under glass avoided the jank that full-size images caused. For cross-origin fixes, I'm considering setting crossOrigin=\"anonymous\" on remote images in liquid mode plus adding CORS headers server-side, with onerror fallback to the CSS card if that fails. As an alternative avoiding header changes, I could have the Electron main process run a local proxy protocol that fetches remote images and serves them same-origin.\n\nDisabling webSecurity entirely is too broad, so instead I'm leaning toward intercepting responses with session.webRequest.onHeadersReceived, filtering for image resource types, and injecting an access-control-allow-origin header only when missing — this adds no new network calls, just loosens what the renderer can read from already-fetched images, which should be safe.\n\nAs for using crossorigin=\"anonymous\" on image tags, that drops cookies, but since the Electron renderer session has no relevant cookies for these hosts anyway (e.g. YouTube thumbnail CDNs don't require auth), this shouldn't break anything, and referrer policy is unaffected by CORS mode. I'll apply crossorigin consistently to all remote images rather than conditionally, since that's simpler and should behave the same in both modes.\n\nHowever, I realize the browser preview mode (`bun run dev`, no Electron main process) has no header injection, so crossorigin on arxiv images would fail there if arxiv doesn't send ACAO — but that mode also doesn't run liquid glass since there's no Electron flag, so maybe crossorigin should only apply when running under Electron. I need to check whether `window.inkling` or similar is defined in the dev:app preview to detect runtime properly.\n\nI could use the presence of `layoutSubtree` on HTMLCanvasElement's prototype as a synchronous proxy check — if that canvas feature flag is enabled, I know we're in the Electron window that also adds the CORS header, since both only exist in that environment. It's a bit implicit, but avoids needing a dedicated preload flag, which might not even load in preview mode anyway. Still, I'm wary this coupling could break if the feature detection changes later.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1884,"cache_read_input_tokens":58446,"output_tokens":3240,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3035},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1884,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3240,"cache_read_input_tokens":58446,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1884},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":34025,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgEkkpnTmtr12MDBXN2Jb","requestRef":"e6e7f7fe-5b4b-4da8-9885-1d4c67fd3495","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"27ba79f8-119f-4695-bd20-c41a65cf59d8","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:31:59.899Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
