{"parentUuid":"1aff3a8f-21f3-4bdc-848c-422af418ceb0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEksznAoU6UacuSakicP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm thinking I should only set crossOrigin on https images for safety, using a small helper that checks if the protocol is \"app:\" and the source is https before returning \"anonymous\". I need to decide where to place this helper—maybe a new file in src/lib, or exported from LiquidGlass.tsx since the cards importing it live in App.tsx and ItemMed.\n\nI'll add it to LiquidGlass.tsx as a canvasSafeCrossOrigin function, since importing it into ItemMedia won't pull in anything heavy. Now I'm writing the main.mjs change, adding a session webRequest handler to strip the Access-Control-Allow-Origin header on image responses so Chromium's crossorigin canvas drawing works correctly despite most hosts lacking CORS headers.\n\nI'm checking whether this affects the default session, confirming only one listener exists per event, and noting this change is harmless for iframe embeds and sanitized reader content since those lack crossorigin attributes. There's a minor edge case where the same image fetched with and without crossorigin creates separate cache entries, but that's negligible. Now I want to look at the renderer code around line 926 in App.tsx to see which component that's in.\n\nBoth image references at 926 and 1083 use item.image, plus the avatar at 206. Overlay flight images render outside the canvas so they don't need changes, but I should check whether the local video asset at line 937 gets painted into the canvas since it's same-origin. Time to write the code.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1796,"cache_read_input_tokens":71464,"output_tokens":1179,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1010},"server_tool_use":{"