{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","prNumber":170,"prUrl":"https://github.com/Squidys-Tools/inkling/pull/170","prRepository":"Squidys-Tools/inkling","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:35:35.112Z"}
{"parentUuid":"0f321836-951a-4e4e-82f1-17a362a2f350","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEm5LQh3FuVRcJ3rmayJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The attention diagram image now renders. I'm checking the glass effects across the UI — the history nav, view controls, and search bar have liquid glass styling, and I'm trying to figure out whether the sidebar's collapse tab gets that glass treatment only in its closed state.\n\nFor the zoom pill in the expanded item overlay, I'm considering whether to wrap the zoomable image stage itself in a LiquidGlass canvas so the pill reads correctly through the backdrop, but I'm wary that rendering full-size, high-resolution images through glass caused noticeable performance hitches before.\n\nThe better approach is a tiny pill-sized LiquidGlass with no shadow, backed by a small replica of the thumbnail positioned to match the measured image and pill rects rather than the full zoomed image. That keeps the canvas small and cheap to redraw as the transform updates during zoom and pan, though I need to think through how refraction behaves at the canvas edges when the glass shape is exactly pill-sized.\n\nSimpler approach: make the canvas exactly pill-sized with CSS clipping handling the rounded border, so the glass shape just fills the whole canvas and edge sampling clamps acceptably.\n\nBut stepping back, I should figure out the full scope of translucent elements that need this treatment — the zoom pill, archive-card-delete, reader bar, note-editor actions, card-video-badge, capture-error, and query-chip all use backdrop-filter currently. I'm cataloging which ones are already converted to the liquid glass approach and which still need it, starting with the library toolbar (done) and working through the rest like query-chip.\n\nI'm also flagging a technical concern: card-video-badge lives inside grid content that gets captured as HTML-in-Canvas, and backdrop-filter may not render correctly there since compositing support for that subtree is uncertain — I'll need to check it visually rather than assume.\n\nFor in-grid badges like video-badge and archive-card-delete, the glass effect can't simply apply to content that's part of its own backdrop, since the icon/text would get refracted or blurred along with it. Properly excluding them and layering glass above would be too complex, so I'll keep these as plain CSS rather than liquid glass.\n\nThe reader view is trickier: wrapping it in LiquidGlass would break remote article images, since they lack crossorigin and would render blank in canvas, plus embedded YouTube iframes don't paint in HTML-to-canvas at all. That's a strong reason to keep the reader bar as CSS glass rather than canvas-based.\n\nFor the zoom pill over the image overlay, I'm leaning toward the replica approach since it's the primary image-viewing surface and worth the extra effort. The concern about large JPEGs causing hitches isn't really about copying the full-resolution image into a texture — it's the per-frame decode/rasterize cost of painting that large image, which a smaller replica element could avoid.\n\nNow I need to work out how the replica tracks the main image's transform: positioning it relative to the pill using the bounding rects, since those already reflect the applied translate/scale. The tricky part is syncing this on every zoom/pan state change, especially with smooth transition animations, which means I likely need a layout effect that recalculates after each render.\n\nI'm weighing whether to generalize the LiquidGlass settle loop with a sync callback, or alternatively skip the replica entirely and let the canvas cover the whole stage — but that means repainting and copying the full-stage texture every frame during panning, which is expensive and comparable to the costly scroll case I was trying to avoid.\n\nThere's also a question of whether the large 6000px image gets re-rasterized per paint in the HTML-in-canvas approach versus using the normal compositor's cached decode — that 120ms hitch might just be the initial decode rather than a repeated cost. I should check how ZoomableImage handles its transitions and look at ExpandedItemOverlay's implementation to clarify this.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1329,"cache_read_input_tokens":84469,"output_tokens":3258,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3074},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1329,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3258,"cache_read_input_tokens":84469,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1329,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1329},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":35197,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgEm5L2NBiageNtQsktB2","requestRef":"29bc3213-a661-449f-875d-120722bfea42","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"0e38769c-2077-4388-a463-aabcfca0a00e","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:36:11.122Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
