{"parentUuid":"d367b720-9b8c-4372-a3d6-04067dbbaa69","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14578103 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"27ef7c45-fc53-4b8c-9fb5-a30dec679b7d","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:46:15.732Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14578103 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","hash":"e37b3022ed3dc1df1262b9bc53101b110ddc6ec9f5aaf75b8a90a783ea026f22","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Some slow frames during resize (50-90ms) are tied to main-thread draws, while others (120-146ms) happen at the end near final canvas width and aren't attributed to draws at all, suggesting layout or GPU work from the virtuoso grid. Since CSS mode only has 4 slow frames across 3 toggles, the canvas resize itself seems to be the real culprit, so I want to figure out what's making those draw calls slow — possibly something in the image copy logic.\n\nResizing a GPU canvas likely forces the swap chain to recreate, which could stall waiting for the GPU to go idle, meaning every resized frame triggers a costly flush. To fix this, I'm considering a bucketed canvas backing size so the canvas doesn't resize every single frame, though a slide crossing bucket boundaries would still cause 1-2 stalls per toggle. Alternatively, I could just allocate the canvas at the maximum possible size (matching the window's full content area when the sidebar is collapsed) so it never needs to shrink or regrow during normal resizing.\n\nPositioning-wise, the canvas and backdrop share the same top-left origin with overflow clipping on the wrapper, which keeps glass shapes aligned. The trickier part is the content div inside the liquid-dom host — since the host matches the allocated (bucketed) size but content needs to render at the actual needed size, I need to explicitly set content's width/height in liquid mode rather than relying on inset-0, while keeping inset-0 for the CSS fallback path and cleaning up the explicit styles on teardown.\n\nThinking through the extra GPU cost from bucket rounding, allocating up to the next 128px bucket adds roughly 10-28% overdraw on average, which is tolerable. The bigger concern is transition stalls — crossing a bucket boundary during resize (like a sidebar toggle growing the stage ~207px) triggers 1-2 reallocation stalls of 50-90ms each, which is a noticeable but much smaller hitch than the ~20 stalls from unbucketed growth.\n\nI'm now considering the opposite approach: simply allocate the canvas at the full window size rather than the stage size, since the stage is always ≤ window dimensions. That would mean sidebar toggles never trigger a realloc at all — only window resizes would, and those can still use grow-only debounced bucketing. The tradeoff is a higher baseline GPU fill cost, roughly +22% for a 1280x720 window versus a 1047x718 open-sidebar stage.\n\nBut that fill cost grows much worse on high-DPI displays — a 4K screen at DPR 2 could push the device canvas to 14.7 megapixels, making every full-canvas pass (blur, displacement, shadow, glass) expensive regardless of the sidebar state. This makes me wonder if liquid-dom's full-canvas passes could instead be limited to just the glass regions rather than blurring the entire backdrop texture, since non-glass areas just pass through the backdrop unchanged anyway.\n\nFor the resize jank problem itself, I'm considering whether to freeze the liquid glass canvas during the sidebar transition rather than resizing live — maybe detecting rapid size changes via ResizeObserver and holding the old allocation temporarily, though stretching the old buffer seems wrong. I should look at how the sidebar animation actually drives the resize to find a better approach.\n\nChecking whether the sidebar collapse animates via CSS grid-template-columns, which would mean the canvas is reflowing every frame regardless — since CSS mode alone stayed smooth, the real bottleneck is specifically the canvas reallocation, not the per-frame reflow. That points toward decoupling the buffer allocation from the live layout size, maybe growing the allocated buffer in rounded increments only when the needed size exceeds it, and leaving it oversized until things settle rather than reallocating on every frame.\n\nActually, maybe the simpler fix is to size the canvas based on the window dimensions rather than the stage, since the stage is always contained within the main window. Sidebar toggles would never trigger a reallocation then, and only actual window resizes would matter—though those already have their own jank in Electron's drag-resize. I'm leaning toward skipping bucketing for window resize and just allocating directly to the window size.\n\nThe extra fill cost from over-allocating to the full window versus the stage is roughly 22% more pixels when the sidebar is open at common widths, but negligible when it's closed—so it seems like an acceptable tradeoff. I'm also wondering whether there's a cheaper way to avoid per-frame reallocation entirely, like treating the canvas as a fixed viewport instead of resizing the WebGPU context's underlying texture each time.\n\nThe 22% extra fill is untested on weak GPUs but probably fine here, and comparing to the grow-only bucketed alternative, that still causes 1-2 visible hitches during sidebar collapse, while the window-size approach avoids any reallocation on toggle entirely — so I'll go with window-size. Now I'm questioning whether the 50-90ms realloc cost comes from re-rastering content into the new texture rather than just the copy itself.\n\nSince liquid-dom only copies changed elements but scroll changes everything, and main-thread scroll cost is cheap (1.4ms median), the stall must be from the GPU-side swapchain resize waiting for idle rather than CPU work. The lingering 120/146ms frames at the final opening width might be from layout remeasurement (virtuoso recalculating card heights) rather than draw cost, since CSS mode doesn't show this — worth implementing and re-measuring to confirm. I'm starting to write the implementation change in LiquidGlass.\n\nWriting resize logic...\n\nI'm realizing the simpler approach of computing allocation from window size and device pixel ratio could cause fractional CSS pixel positioning, which risks blurring the canvas due to subpixel misalignment—this is exactly why devicePixelContentBoxSize matters for crisp rendering.\n\nI'm realizing the ResizeObserver loop should work fine since it fires rarely, only when CSS size changes. Now I'm thinking through a separate wrapper observer that tracks content box size for allocation purposes, trying to figure out how the allocation CSS size should be derived—maybe rounding window-based dimensions up to a fixed step to avoid excessive reallocation.\n\nAn even cleaner approach occurs to me: just size the canvas with pure CSS at 100vw/100vh so no JS is needed for allocation at all, letting the ResizeObserver on the canvas handle backing size from device pixels. The content size still needs JS from a wrapper observer, though, since CSS alone can't size content to match a different containing block.\n\nWindow drag-resizes will cause per-frame canvas reallocation, same as current behavior, so I'll accept that jank since it's less noticeable than animation jank. I also realize the canvas at 100vw/100vh extends past the stage's offset position, but overflow hidden on the wrapper clips the extra pixels, so it's just a bit of wasted allocation, not a visual bug.\n\nFor the ResizeObserver setup in LiquidGlass, I'm setting explicit width/height on the content element only in liquid glass mode, leaving CSS-based inset:0 positioning for fallback mode. I need to be careful that setting an explicit width alongside inset:0 positioning causes the right/bottom edges to be ignored due to over-constraint in LTR layouts.\n\nThe observer callback order matters—canvas backing size and wrapper content size fire together, then shape syncing follows, and since RO runs before paint, the layout changes trigger re-layout within the same frame before the actual paint event fires. I'm also verifying that glass shapes stay correctly positioned relative to the canvas's top-left corner since the canvas sits flush at the wrapper's origin, which keeps the culling logic for off-canvas shapes working correctly.\n\nI'm sizing the canvas to the window rather than the stage to avoid resize-triggered GPU stalls during sidebar animations, using 100vw/100vh with overflow hidden on the stage container. Setting up the resize observer logic...\n\nIn fallback mode the content sits inset within the wrapper, so explicit sizing isn't needed there. But there's a subtle initial-frame bug: before the ResizeObserver fires, the host starts at a tiny 1x1 size, so the content briefly collapses, causing the virtualized list to render as if the viewport were 1x1 before correcting itself.\n\nTo avoid that flash, I'm thinking I should set the content's size synchronously from the wrapper's bounding rect right before constructing the surface, rather than waiting for the observer to catch up.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The slow frames align with canvas resizes—each sidebar slide triggers a WebGPU reallocation costing 50–90ms per frame. I'll fix this by sizing the canvas to the window (which rarely changes) and laying out content at the stage's size within it.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011RbdPzYH6eDvN3KSmf4ZuP","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /c/Users/chris/.t3/worktrees/inkling/t3-47ff5304 && python - <<'EOF'\np='src/components/LiquidGlass.tsx'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\ndef rep(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, old[:70]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\nrep('''    let live = true;\n    let shown = false;\n    const marked = new Set<HTMLElement>();\n    const surface''','''    let live = true;\n    let shown = false;\n    const marked = new Set<HTMLElement>();\n    // The content lays out at the wrapper's size. The canvas can be larger (see\n    // .library-stage in App.css), so the content no longer fills its parent.\n    const sizeContent = (width: number, height: number) => {\n      content.style.width = `${width}px`;\n      content.style.height = `${height}px`;\n    };\n    sizeContent(wrap.clientWidth, wrap.clientHeight);\n    const surface''')\nrep('''    const sizeObserver = new ResizeObserver((entries) => {\n      for (const entry of entries) {\n        if (entry.target !== canvas) continue;\n        const box = entry.contentBoxSize[0];\n        const device = entry.devicePixelContentBoxSize?.[0];\n        if (!box) continue;\n        surface.resize(''','''    const sizeObserver = new ResizeObserver((entries) => {\n      for (const entry of entries) {\n        const box = entry.contentBoxSize[0];\n        if (!box) continue;\n        if (entry.target === wrap) sizeContent(box.inlineSize, box.blockSize);\n        if (entry.target !== canvas) continue;\n        const device = entry.devicePixelContentBoxSize?.[0];\n        surface.resize(''')\nrep('''    sizeObserver.observe(canvas);\n''','''    sizeObserver.observe(canvas);\n    sizeObserver.observe(wrap);\n''')\nrep('''      surface.destroy();\n      // Back to plain DOM in the wrapper.\n      wrap.append(content);''','''      surface.destroy();\n      // Back to plain DOM in the wrapper.\n      content.style.width = \"\";\n      content.style.height = \"\";\n      wrap.append(content);''')\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8',newline='').write(s)\n\np='src/App.css'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\nrep('''.library-stage {\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0;\n  z-index: 0;\n}''','''.library-stage {\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0;\n  z-index: 0;\n  overflow: hidden;\n}\n/* Sized to the window, not the stage. Resizing a WebGPU canvas stalls the GPU\n   for tens of milliseconds, and the stage resizes every frame of a sidebar\n   slide; the window rarely does. The extra edge is clipped. */\n.library-stage .liquid-glass-canvas {\n  width: 100vw;\n  height: 100vh;\n}''')\nrep('''.liquid-glass-canvas {\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0;''','''.liquid-glass-canvas {\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 0;\n  left: 0;''')\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8',newline='').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"^\\.liquid-glass-canvas\" -A 8 src/App.css","description":"Size library canvas to window, content to stage"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
