{"type":"api-request","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","id":"bd49655a-33a8-4ffc-b302-25aca0ac936b","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:48:35.728Z","version":"2.1.305","querySource":"sdk","shapeHash":"a9ed8921dc21a185c51f032ab201ef86db193f2b4a3c27f0d2631dc37b2aa860","params":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","betas":["claude-code-20250219","oauth-2025-04-20","interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14","thinking-token-count-2026-05-13","context-management-2025-06-27","prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05","per-turn-control-2026-07-01","mid-conversation-tool-changes-2026-07-01","inline-tools-2026-09-15","advisor-tool-2026-03-01","advanced-tool-use-2025-11-20","mid-conversation-system-clear-at-2026-08-21","effort-2025-11-24","thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01","extended-cache-ttl-2025-04-11","cache-diagnosis-2026-04-07","message-threads-2026-08-12"],"max_tokens":128000,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"summarized"},"context_management":{"edits":[{"type":"clear_thinking_20251015","keep":"all"}]},"output_config":{"effort":"xhigh"}},"base":"402edb33-cc5d-4699-86e9-bf087af66378","keep":191,"tail":["b158e5e43cddf7a2f3d07f1913469d8113fc0e014c1c254b6ba2c0be148524d1","f31c74447e2299643ed9de81fac86df758d447eb5156df313414f9d62045281e","03e0b0d8ed399a0b4107a86a819b640db9d84a10e3059ba2d7134484b514d207"],"cacheControl":[{"path":["system",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h","scope":"global"}},{"path":["system",2],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",193,"content",0],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"requestDigest":"399b296f96391e4a850bc4b43505ad177fce4d8ce4911f1b160ff8f94479c705"}
{"parentUuid":"3c4e278c-5a72-42ac-b42f-02e470ae13d4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEn4t1ZbTwWhKAFV3vou","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I realize translating the scene transform would also move the DOM host via CSS, breaking hit testing, so that's not usable directly. I'm now considering an alternative architecture where the canvas itself is shrunk to just the strip, rendering the library content as normal DOM instead of relying on scene transforms.\n\nI'm also weighing patching liquid-dom's `drawFrame` to add a scissor rect, but WebGPU's clear loadOp isn't scissored, so it would clear the entire attachment and require complex blit logic to preserve content outside the strip. As a less invasive option, I could implement a minimal custom content-source object that supplies its own texture and inverse transform for the Html compositing layer, avoiding changes to liquid-dom itself.\n\nWeighing cost versus benefit, the sidebar jank (37fps during a 300ms slide) is brief but would be worse on weaker GPUs or 4K displays, so the strip optimization — cutting roughly 10 full-screen passes down to one full copy plus ~10 small strip passes covering about 10% of the area — seems worth the 5-8x GPU savings. I also need to check whether the pinned query chip, positioned near the top strip, is accounted for in this optimization.\n\nI'm defining the strip region as the union bounding box of all glass shapes expanded by a margin (around 48px, to cover shadow blur, offset, and refraction sampling), clamped to the canvas and recomputed whenever shapes change — falling back to a plain content copy if no shapes exist. I'm also confirming that the 14px blur radius stays within this margin so the blur chain doesn't clip near strip edges.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to build a custom content source for the strip core, checking what `contentSource` interface fields are needed (`getSceneHtmlEntry`, binding resources, atlas texture) and how `compositeHtmlLayer` consumes entry fields like texture, transform, and dimensions — planning to reuse the real layer entries but return a modified copy with an adjusted inverse transform to account for the strip's offset.\n\nSimpler alternative: instead of translating every layer's transform at render time, I could just make the glass shapes strip-relative from the start by setting the Container's x/y to the negative strip offset directly in the scene definition.\n\nFor the HTML backdrop though, since I can't move its DOM node, I need to compose the inverse transform with the strip offset so the content source wrapper correctly maps device pixels back to local HTML coordinates.\n\nThinking about scope: this is roughly 60 lines in LiquidSurface, and the main risk is relying on internal duck-typed APIs from the content source. Since it's liquid-dom's own WebGpuDomContentSource, I can wrap it with a Proxy that overrides just getSceneHtmlEntry while delegating everything else through.\n\nFor the output path, the core renders into my own intermediate strip texture, then I copy the full content entry texture into the canvas texture and the strip texture into the canvas at the right offset — doing this in my own encoder after the core's submit so queue ordering guarantees correctness. I'm now double-checking whether the content entry texture actually matches the canvas device size or if it's padded differently.\n\nI need to confirm format compatibility across the html texture, strip texture, and canvas, plus ensure the source textures actually have COPY_SRC usage set so copyTextureToTexture won't fail.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":756,"cache_read_input_tokens":169497,"output_tokens":3060,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2828},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"s