{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","hash":"4500bd16e9d33dedc5049583083397bb41adcab20c3c082d44fca76ab34d0189","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Since WebGpuGlassContentSource is just a plain object type, I realize I can construct my own object that proxies the properties from the DOM content source, since it implements the interface and exposes the needed members like atlasTexture.\n\nNext I want to write the glassBand pure function, which computes which device-pixel rows of the canvas contain glass shapes in CSS pixels, accounting for margin and texture reallocation steps. I'm also checking that capture-error and query-chip elements stay near the top of the capture bar.\n\nWriting band calculation function...\n\nI'm tracing through a few examples to verify the band-clamping logic: no shapes returns zero, a toolbar pill rounds up to a 256px band, a lens shape clamps to the canvas height, and a shape positioned far down the canvas produces the correct offset band within bounds.\n\nNow I'm shifting to the LiquidSurface changes — configuring context usage for render attachment and copy destination, and setting the container's y-offset based on the band before sync() runs, since the layers' transform depends on that being set beforehand. I'm also double-checking that updating container.y won't incorrectly trigger a full DOM content recopy during sync.\n\nWriting draw function...\n\nStill writing texture copy logic...\n\nThe area outside content within the html host should stay transparent against the opaque canvas, properly clipped by the wrapper. For the band rendering, I'm checking that the shader correctly maps fragment coordinates back through the shift to sample html local coordinates. I'm also verifying packShapes handles the container transform (translation and dpr scaling) correctly, and whether the core needs any Y-flip relative to height for glass shapes.\n\nBackdrop metrics for adaptive specular only matter if that readback feature is enabled, so I can skip it here. I'm defining a bandTarget helper that recreates the band texture when dimensions change, plus a destroy method to clean up the texture. The output texture just needs RENDER_ATTACHMENT usage since the core renders into it directly, and resize() correctly triggers a repaint.\n\nsetShapes also triggers requestPaint since the band gets recomputed during draw, and I'll keep the existing first-frame callback logic that checks for a copied device width before firing onFirstFrame. Now I'll write the actual code.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll use a small wrapper object around the public `WebGpuGlassContentSource` type for the shifted backdrop, then write the band renderer with the band math as a tested pure function.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AWrXst8hopbhwg6AYA3B5w","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /c/Users/chris/.t3/worktrees/inkling/t3-47ff5304 && cat >> src/components/liquidGlassShape.ts <<'EOF'\n\n// The drop shadow (8px down, 24px blur) and refraction reach this far past a\n// shape, in CSS pixels.\nconst BAND_MARGIN = 64;\n// Device pixels. The band's texture is only reallocated when its height crosses\n// a step, so a chip sliding in does not reallocate it every frame.\nconst BAND_STEP = 128;\n\n/**\n * The device-pixel rows of a canvas that hold glass. liquid-dom runs a dozen\n * full-size passes per frame, so only these rows go through it; the rest of the\n * canvas is a plain copy of the content.\n */\nexport function glassBand(\n  shapes: Iterable<{ y: number; height: number }>,\n  dpr: number,\n  canvasHeight: number,\n) {\n  let minY = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;\n  let maxY = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;\n  for (const shape of shapes) {\n    minY = Math.min(minY, shape.y);\n    maxY = Math.max(maxY, shape.y + shape.height);\n  }\n  if (minY > maxY) return { top: 0, height: 0 };\n  const top = Math.max(0, Math.floor((minY - BAND_MARGIN) * dpr));\n  const rows = Math.ceil((maxY + BAND_MARGIN) * dpr) - top;\n  const height = Math.min(canvasHeight - top, Math.ceil(rows / BAND_STEP) * BAND_STEP);\n  return { top, height: Math.max(0, height) };\n}\nEOF\ncat >> src/components/liquidGlassShape.test.ts <<'EOF'\n\ntest(\"no glass means no band\", () => {\n  expect(glassBand([], 1, 720)).toEqual({ top: 0, height: 0 });\n});\n\ntest(\"toolbar glass gets a band from the top, rounded up to a step\", () => {\n  expect(\n    glassBand(\n      [\n        { y: 45, height: 34 },\n        { y: 39, height: 34 },\n      ],\n      1,\n      720,\n    ),\n  ).toEqual({ top: 0, height: 256 });\n});\n\ntest(\"a band never runs past the canvas\", () => {\n  expect(glassBand([{ y: -1, height: 38 }], 1.5, 54)).toEqual({ top: 0, height: 54 });\n  expect(glassBand([{ y: 500, height: 30 }], 1, 720)).toEqual({ top: 436, height: 256 });\n  expect(glassBand([{ y: 600, height: 30 }], 1, 720)).toEqual({ top: 536, height: 184 });\n});\nEOF\nsed -i 's|import { glassShape } from \"./liquidGlassShape\";|import { glassBand, glassShape } from \"./liquidGlassShape\";|' src/components/liquidGlassShape.test.ts && bun test src/components/liquidGlassShape.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -4","description":"Add glassBand helper and tests"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
