{"parentUuid":"eff44b4d-29af-44a1-ad74-47971df5aa16","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEnucMqq7nLkpYiAGuUj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Tq1L1H3eZ472noUUE3gaiW","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 11,25p docs/tech-stack.md; sed -n 100,110p docs/tech-stack.md; awk '/^## Unreleased/{f=1} f' docs/changelog.md | sed -n 3,12p; echo ...; awk '/^## Unreleased/{f=1} f' docs/changelog.md | sed -n 40,60p","description":"Read stack and security sections, changelog spots"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1229,"cache_read_input_tokens":234197,"output_tokens":234,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1229,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":234,"cache_read_input_tokens":234197,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1229,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1229},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Tq1L1H3eZ472noUUE3gaiW":{"command":"cd /c/Users/chris/.t3/worktrees/inkling/t3-47ff5304 && sed -n 11,25p docs/tech-stack.md; sed -n 100,110p docs/tech-stack.md; awk '/^## Unreleased/{f=1} f' docs/changelog.md | sed -n 3,12p; echo ...; awk '/^## Unreleased/{f=1} f' docs/changelog.md | sed -n 40,60p","description":"Read stack and security sections, changelog spots"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01Tq1L1H3eZ472noUUE3gaiW":{"cwd":"/c/Users/chris/.t3/worktrees/inkling/t3-47ff5304"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgEnubfvA2PHp9bsSgG1U","requestRef":"aa1bcfa9-68ac-48f8-ba66-fe8beb9de72f","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"9c95066b-0baa-4899-be89-dd975f7b0cfa","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:59:39.204Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"9c95066b-0baa-4899-be89-dd975f7b0cfa","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a5d551ad-5e9e-431c-a603-b554797c9ccd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tq1L1H3eZ472noUUE3gaiW","type":"tool_result","content":"## Application stack\n\n| Area | Decision | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Desktop shell | Electron 44 | Windows desktop packaging with a reusable web frontend and a narrow preload bridge |\n| UI | React + TypeScript + Vite | Mature component ecosystem and future web portability |\n| Styling | Tailwind CSS plus accessible headless primitives | Fast iteration without locking the product to a heavy component theme |\n| Client state | Zustand | Small, explicit state layer for UI state |\n| Server-style data state | TanStack Query | Caching and invalidation for asynchronous repository calls |\n| Application core | Electron main process and Node.js | File, job queue, database, and model-process orchestration |\n| Database | SQLite through `node:sqlite` | Direct control over migrations, transactions, and FTS5 |\n| Exact search | SQLite FTS5 | Local full-text search across content, notes, OCR, and summaries |\n| Asset storage | Content-addressed local files | Avoid duplicate assets and make backup/restore predictable |\n| Background work | Node.js job queue persisted in SQLite | Retryable processing without blocking the UI |\n\n3. Accelerated mode when a supported GPU is available.\n\n## Data, backup, and security\n\nThe database and asset store should be exportable together. Backups should include the SQLite database, local assets, original source URLs, cleaned article content, user notes and tags, and Spaces. Derived AI data may be regenerated, but exporting it is useful for faster restore and future migration.\n\nTreat all fetched HTML as untrusted. Sanitize article HTML before rendering, allowlist iframe providers, strip scripts/event handlers/unsafe URLs/untrusted `srcdoc`, keep remote content isolated from Electron's privileged main process, and do not expose arbitrary filesystem or shell commands through the preload bridge.\n\n## Update checks\n\nThe packaged Windows app checks GitHub Releases once when it opens; it does not poll on a schedule. GitHub receives the request from the device and the app name and installed version in its HTTP user agent, but no library data. An available update appears quietly in the sidebar and is downloaded and installed only after the user selects it; that action checks once more before downloading. Electron updater hashes protect update downloads over HTTPS but do not establish publisher trust. The NSIS installer is not Authenticode-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may warn users when they install it.\n### Added\n\n- First-run welcome flow on first launch of the installed app, replayable from Settings → General\n- PDF cards now show page-one thumbnails generated in the background after capture; existing PDFs are backfilled one at a time when the job queue is idle.\n- Chrome MV3 extension saves a page or right-clicked link through inkling's local capture receiver, with the existing capture link as fallback.\n- `bun run ui:smoke` drives the core library flows in Electron against a scratch profile and fails on console errors\n- Electron desktop shell alongside Tauri, with scoped `app://` assets, an IPC command allowlist, and `inkling://` capture links.\n- Electron now opens the shared schema-v8 SQLite library with search, Spaces, archive, backup/restore, and a persisted job queue running local OCR, PDF extraction, and Nomic embeddings.\n- Electron now accepts authenticated browser-extension saves over a loopback server and stores URL deep links before processing runs.\n- Electron now ships with a Windows NSIS installer, bundled local models, and a generic GitHub Release update feed; unsigned installers and updates may trigger SmartScreen warnings.\n...\n- The mascot wanders the library on its own: 90 to 180 seconds after startup, and again after a long cooldown, it leaves the sidebar slot and drifts through the app, wearing a random expression at each stop and looking toward cards and recent pointer activity before going back. It never travels between points: it is given a direction and left to get on with it, with a turn rate that makes a change of mind a curve and a very small sway layered on the heading, so the path is unhurried, mostly straight and never the same twice. It never goes over the card grid - that is a wall it slides along rather than a filter on where it may stop - and the rest of the app, the sidebar and the strips of controls included, is somewhere it is allowed to be. It wanders in one part for a few seconds before it will consider blinking somewhere else, and it squashes out and swells up somewhere new and sets off again. It watches what the user does without being cancelled by it, holds still under overlays, dialogs, the expanded item, Serendipity and an in-flight drag, goes home sad when a capture fails, and never leaves home under reduced motion, plus a dev board at `?mascot-roam` (SQU-58) (`src/components/mascot/roam.ts`, `src/components/mascot/drift.ts`, `src/components/mascot/roamSpace.ts`, `src/components/mascot/roamStore.ts`)\n- Regular Spaces: manual collections alongside saved searches. The Space create form chooses Smart or Regular, a Regular Space holds only the items you file into it from the expanded item view, and the chips there show current membership and take an item back out. Deleting a Space still leaves its items alone (SQU-4, `src-tauri/src/storage.rs`, `src/App.tsx`, `src/components/ExpandedItemOverlay.tsx`)\n- Top of Mind: items can be pinned and unpinned from their details view, and the Top of mind view collects them. That view explains itself when nothing is pinned yet, and the main library stays a plain list of everything (SQU-9) (`src/App.tsx`, `src/components/ExpandedItemOverlay.tsx`)\n- The item count above the library rolls up when it first appears and replays when hovered, using the React Bits CountUp in compact format (`src/components/CountUp.tsx`, `src/components/AnimatedCount.tsx`)\n\n### Fixed\n\n- URL captures from Add, paste, deep links, and the browser extension now appear immediately and enrich the same item through the retryable local job queue.\n- The app no longer contacts Google Fonts or Twitter/X at startup: reader fonts are bundled with the app and the content security policy no longer allows Google Fonts requests\n- X post embeds load the X widget only when the user clicks to load the post, so cards and saved links no longer contact X or Twitter on open\n- The library bottom fade no longer shows a hard edge where the blur band starts or where it meets the padding below the grid: one blur layer feathers out through an eased mask, and a canvas-colored scrim dissolves content into the background at the grid's clip line (#149)\n\n### Changed\n\n- Library list IPC sends card fields only; reader content is loaded from SQLite when opened.\n- Electron defers canvas system-font loading until PDF work runs; BMP and ICO thumbnails use canvas without loading system fonts.\n- Library cards form a denser masonry grid with tighter gutters and natural media proportions; note and quote previews stay readable, while list, Archive, and Serendipity views keep their existing geometry.\n- Desktop window opens at 960 × 540 instead of 1000 × 800; the 320 × 360 minimum size is unchanged.\n- Developer experience: `tsc-rs` is the blocking typecheck gate for the root, benchmark, extension, and ingestion-shared projects, including the build scripts, and real `tsc` runs as a non-blocking CI canary (`typecheck:tsc`).\n- Developer commands share one namespace: `dev:app` runs the desktop app, `build:installer` builds the Windows installer, and `check` runs every gate; the old `electron:*`, `check:frontend`, and `knip:check` names are removed.\n- Biome now formats and lints project files locally, and frontend CI enforces those checks (#109).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e5c310fb-7a94-4487-ab2c-2eea0f543960","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:59:39.962Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## Application stack\n\n| Area | Decision | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Desktop shell | Electron 44 | Windows desktop packaging with a reusable web frontend and a narrow preload bridge |\n| UI | React + TypeScript + Vite | Mature component ecosystem and future web portability |\n| Styling | Tailwind CSS plus accessible headless primitives | Fast iteration without locking the product to a heavy component theme |\n| Client state | Zustand | Small, explicit state layer for UI state |\n| Server-style data state | TanStack Query | Caching and invalidation for asynchronous repository calls |\n| Application core | Electron main process and Node.js | File, job queue, database, and model-process orchestration |\n| Database | SQLite through `node:sqlite` | Direct control over migrations, transactions, and FTS5 |\n| Exact search | SQLite FTS5 | Local full-text search across content, notes, OCR, and summaries |\n| Asset storage | Content-addressed local files | Avoid duplicate assets and make backup/restore predictable |\n| Background work | Node.js job queue persisted in SQLite | Retryable processing without blocking the UI |\n\n3. Accelerated mode when a supported GPU is available.\n\n## Data, backup, and security\n\nThe database and asset store should be exportable together. Backups should include the SQLite database, local assets, original source URLs, cleaned article content, user notes and tags, and Spaces. Derived AI data may be regenerated, but exporting it is useful for faster restore and future migration.\n\nTreat all fetched HTML as untrusted. Sanitize article HTML before rendering, allowlist iframe providers, strip scripts/event handlers/unsafe URLs/untrusted `srcdoc`, keep remote content isolated from Electron's privileged main process, and do not expose arbitrary filesystem or shell commands through the preload bridge.\n\n## Update checks\n\nThe packaged Windows app checks GitHub Releases once when it opens; it does not poll on a schedule. GitHub receives the request from the device and the app name and installed version in its HTTP user agent, but no library data. An available update appears quietly in the sidebar and is downloaded and installed only after the user selects it; that action checks once more before downloading. Electron updater hashes protect update downloads over HTTPS but do not establish publisher trust. The NSIS installer is not Authenticode-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may warn users when they install it.\n### Added\n\n- First-run welcome flow on first launch of the installed app, replayable from Settings → General\n- PDF cards now show page-one thumbnails generated in the background after capture; existing PDFs are backfilled one at a time when the job queue is idle.\n- Chrome MV3 extension saves a page or right-clicked link through inkling's local capture receiver, with the existing capture link as fallback.\n- `bun run ui:smoke` drives the core library flows in Electron against a scratch profile and fails on console errors\n- Electron desktop shell alongside Tauri, with scoped `app://` assets, an IPC command allowlist, and `inkling://` capture links.\n- Electron now opens the shared schema-v8 SQLite library with search, Spaces, archive, backup/restore, and a persisted job queue running local OCR, PDF extraction, and Nomic embeddings.\n- Electron now accepts authenticated browser-extension saves over a loopback server and stores URL deep links before processing runs.\n- Electron now ships with a Windows NSIS installer, bundled local models, and a generic GitHub Release update feed; unsigned installers and updates may trigger SmartScreen warnings.\n...\n- The mascot wanders the library on its own: 90 to 180 seconds after startup, and again after a long cooldown, it leaves the sidebar slot and drifts through the app, wearing a random expression at each stop and looking toward cards and recent pointer activity before going back. It never travels between points: it is given a direction and left to get on with it, with a turn rate that makes a change of mind a curve and a very small sway layered on the heading, so the path is unhurried, mostly straight and never the same twice. It never goes over the card grid - that is a wall it slides along rather than a filter on where it may stop - and the rest of the app, the sidebar and the strips of controls included, is somewhere it is allowed to be. It wanders in one part for a few seconds before it will consider blinking somewhere else, and it squashes out and swells up somewhere new and sets off again. It watches what the user does without being cancelled by it, holds still under overlays, dialogs, the expanded item, Serendipity and an in-flight drag, goes home sad when a capture fails, and never leaves home under reduced motion, plus a dev board at `?mascot-roam` (SQU-58) (`src/components/mascot/roam.ts`, `src/components/mascot/drift.ts`, `src/components/mascot/roamSpace.ts`, `src/components/mascot/roamStore.ts`)\n- Regular Spaces: manual collections alongside saved searches. The Space create form chooses Smart or Regular, a Regular Space holds only the items you file into it from the expanded item view, and the chips there show current membership and take an item back out. Deleting a Space still leaves its items alone (SQU-4, `src-tauri/src/storage.rs`, `src/App.tsx`, `src/components/ExpandedItemOverlay.tsx`)\n- Top of Mind: items can be pinned and unpinned from their details view, and the Top of mind view collects them. That view explains itself when nothing is pinned yet, and the main library stays a plain list of everything (SQU-9) (`src/App.tsx`, `src/components/ExpandedItemOverlay.tsx`)\n- The item count above the library rolls up when it first appears and replays when hovered, using the React Bits CountUp in compact format (`src/components/CountUp.tsx`, `src/components/AnimatedCount.tsx`)\n\n### Fixed\n\n- URL captures from Add, paste, deep links, and the browser extension now appear immediately and enrich the same item through the retryable local job queue.\n- The app no longer contacts Google Fonts or Twitter/X at startup: reader fonts are bundled with the app and the content security policy no longer allows Google Fonts requests\n- X post embeds load the X widget only when the user clicks to load the post, so cards and saved links no longer contact X or Twitter on open\n- The library bottom fade no longer shows a hard edge where the blur band starts or where it meets the padding below the grid: one blur layer feathers out through an eased mask, and a canvas-colored scrim dissolves content into the background at the grid's clip line (#149)\n\n### Changed\n\n- Library list IPC sends card fields only; reader content is loaded from SQLite when opened.\n- Electron defers canvas system-font loading until PDF work runs; BMP and ICO thumbnails use canvas without loading system fonts.\n- Library cards form a denser masonry grid with tighter gutters and natural media proportions; note and quote previews stay readable, while list, Archive, and Serendipity views keep their existing geometry.\n- Desktop window opens at 960 × 540 instead of 1000 × 800; the 320 × 360 minimum size is unchanged.\n- Developer experience: `tsc-rs` is the blocking typecheck gate for the root, benchmark, extension, and ingestion-shared projects, including the build scripts, and real `tsc` runs as a non-blocking CI canary (`typecheck:tsc`).\n- Developer commands share one namespace: `dev:app` runs the desktop app, `build:installer` builds the Windows installer, and `check` runs every gate; the old `electron:*`, `check:frontend`, and `knip:check` names are removed.\n- Biome now formats and lints project files locally, and frontend CI enforces those checks (#109).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9c95066b-0baa-4899-b