{"parentUuid":"777d9389-8580-4b58-8cd7-bb92fdbd54ef","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgFNgiLbsgvTsRveeZWoB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Since the library stage renders into a canvas, backdrop-filter effects still apply to it as a normal DOM element, which matters for how the liquid glass effect layers with the bottom fade currently handled separately in main. I'm working out the scroller's positioning so it aligns with the blue reference lines in the screenshot, accounting for device pixel scaling and titlebar height.\n\nI need to adjust padding to compensate for the shift so the rest position stays stable, and for the pinned query case I'll keep the top unchanged since the filter row would otherwise overlap the grid — only the bottom extends.\n\nFor replacing the hard cutoff with a progressive blur that isn't dark, I'm realizing a plain mask-fade just reveals the dark frame background underneath, which isn't really the same as removing a dark overlay — so \"not dark\" likely means something else entirely, and I need to reconsider what approach actually avoids darkening.\n\nLooking at how Paper does this, it seems like a lighter blur with fading content rather than a tinted overlay. I'm thinking I'll combine backdrop-filter blur layers at the edges with a mask on the scroller that softens just the last bit near the clip line, rather than a hard cut. I should check the Paper file directly to see their exact implementation before committing to this approach.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2732,"cache_read_input_tokens":97419,"output_tokens":1230,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1100},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2732,"ephemeral_5m_input