{"parentUuid":"93eab9cd-0140-4a35-974a-c8211010f672","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304\\src\\components\\LibraryEdgeFade.tsx","snippet":"2\t\n3\t// The grid scrolls almost to the frame's edges; these blur it out there instead\n4\t// of a hard clip. They render in the liquid glass backdrop, so the toolbar glass\n5\t// refracts the blur rather than being blurred itself. Values are the React Bits\n6\t// \"Subtle\" preset as tuned on reactbits.dev; the tint is #120f17 at its 0.3\n7\t// tint amount. 5 layers instead of 8: it looks the same, and each layer is\n8\t// another backdrop blur on every scrolled frame.\n9\tconst EDGE_BLUR: GradualBlurProps = {\n10\t  height: \"6rem\",\n11\t  strength: 1.5,\n12\t  layers: 5,\n13\t  curve: \"ease-in\",\n14\t  exponential: true,\n15\t  opacity: 1,\n16\t  saturation: 1,\n17\t  tint: \"rgba(18, 15, 23, 0.15)\",\n18\t  hoverIntensity: 0,\n19\t  autoHide: false,\n20\t  zIndex: 1,\n21\t  className: \"library-edge-blur\",\n22\t};\n23\t\n24\texport function LibraryEdgeFade() {\n25\t  return (\n26\t    <>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c2b93145-6739-466a-8b2d-26fe8f5dd778","timestamp":"2026-10-21T13:46:12.168Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304\\src\\components\\LibraryEdgeFade.tsx changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n2\t\n3\t// The grid scrolls almost to the frame's edges; these blur it out there instead\n4\t// of a hard clip. They render in the liquid glass backdrop, so the toolbar glass\n5\t// refracts the blur rather than being blurred itself. Values are the React Bits\n6\t// \"Subtle\" preset as tuned on reactbits.dev; the tint is #120f17 at its 0.3\n7\t// tint amount. 5 layers instead of 8: it looks the same, and each layer is\n8\t// another backdrop blur on every scrolled frame.\n9\tconst EDGE_BLUR: GradualBlurProps = {\n10\t  height: \"6rem\",\n11\t  strength: 1.5,\n12\t  layers: 5,\n13\t  curve: \"ease-in\",\n14\t  exponential: true,\n15\t  opacity: 1,\n16\t  saturation: 1,\n17\t  tint: \"rgba(18, 15, 23, 0.15)\",\n18\t  hoverIntensity: 0,\n19\t  autoHide: false,\n20\t  zIndex: 1,\n21\t  className: \"library-edge-blur\",\n22\t};\n23\t\n24\texport function LibraryEdgeFade() {\n25\t  return (\n26\t    <>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.306","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
